"OAI Teen Safety Blueprint.docx (https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/OAI%20Teen%20Safety%20Blueprint.pdf)\nciteturn2316view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 4; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn2314view1\",\"id\":5}); Total lines: 120\nL0@P0: Protecting Teen ChatGPT Users:\nL1@P0: OpenAI’s Teen Safety Blueprint\nL2@P0: November 2025\nL3@P0: At OpenAI, we believe that access to safe and trustworthy AI should be seen as a right, being a\nL4@P0: revolutionary technology that will help people unlock their potential and shape their future. We also\nL5@P0: believe society has a window in which to approach AI differently from previous revolutionary\nL6@P0: technologies – social media in particular – for which both the public and private sector considered\nL7@P0: the impacts on our children long after the technology had been widely adopted.\nL8@P0: As the first generation to come of age in the Intelligence Age, today’s teens should have access to\nL9@P0: safe and trustworthy AI at home, at school, and as they prepare to join the workforce, and they\nL10@P0: should be protected from its potential harms. Teens are growing up with AI but aren’t grown-ups\nL11@P0: yet. We believe ChatGPT should meet them where they are: the way ChatGPT responds to a\nL12@P0: 15-year-old should differ from the way it responds to an adult. As our CEO Sam Altman has said,\nL13@P0: for teens, we prioritize safety ahead of privacy and freedom. This is a new and powerful\nL14@P0: technology, and we believe minors need significant protection.\nL15@P0: That’s why we’re taking steps to strengthen protections for teens through new age prediction,\nL16@P0: age-appropriate policies, and parental controls, informed by conversations with policymakers,\nL17@P0: including state attorneys general, and with experts, some of whom have joined our new Expert\nL18@P0-1: Council on Well-Being and AI.These protections come in addition to features already available for all users, including in-app\nL19@P1: reminders during long sessions to encourage breaks; safeguards that direct users to real-world\nL20@P1: resources if we detect they have expressed suicidal intent; ways to escalate risks of physical harm\nL21@P1: to others for human reviewers who can take action; and industry-leading prevention of\nL22@P1: AI-generated child sexual abuse material (CSAM) and child sexual exploitation material (CSEM). In\nL23@P1: particular, we constantly improve our CSAM/CSEM detection methods and report confirmed\nL24@P1: CSAM to relevant authorities, including the National Center for Missing and Exploited Children\nL25@P1: (NCMEC). In the first half of 2025, we reported more than 75,000 cybertips to NCMEC.\nL26@P1: Going forward, we aim to ensure that all teens using AI receive age-appropriate protections by\nL27@P1: default, and that parents and educators can deploy additional protections to personalize how teens\nL28@P1: use AI. We encourage all AI companies to prioritize teen safety over freedom and privacy and\nL29@P1: encourage others to implement commensurate protections for teens. To us, that means:\nL30@P1: 1. Identifying teens on our platforms to treat teens like teens and adults like adults.\nL31@P1: We believe that AI companies should identify teens on their platforms using privacy-protective,\nL32@P1: risk-based age estimation tools to distinguish between teens and adults. These tools should\nL33@P1: minimize the collection of sensitive personal data, while still effectively distinguishing users under\nL34@P1: the age of 18 (U18). Where possible, these methods might also rely on operating systems or app\nL35@P1: stores to determine a user’s age. Age estimation will help AI companies ensure that they are\nL36@P1: applying the right protections to the right users. It will facilitate age-appropriate experiences and\nL37@P1: allow AI companies to treat teens like teens and adults like adults.\nL38@P1: 2. Identifying and mitigating risks to minors through under-18 safety policies.\nL39@P1: Teens have specific developmental needs that differ from adults. We believe that AI companies\nL40@P1: should recognize this and not cut corners when it comes to teen safety and well-being. AI systems\nL41@P1: should be designed with age-appropriate protections by default. This means that AI companies\nL42@P1: should have safety policies for U18 users that facilitate age-appropriate interactions. These policies\nL43@P1: should be transparent and informed by research. We believe that safety policies should aim to\nL44@P1: ensure that for U18 users, AI systems:\nL45@P1: ● Do not depict suicide or self-harm.\nL46@P1: ● Prohibit graphic or immersive (i.e., role-playing) intimate and violent content.\nL47@P1: ● Do not instruct, encourage, or facilitate (i.e., shopping links) dangerous stunts such as the\nL48@P1: TidePod or Benadryl challenges, or help minors access dangerous or illegal substances.\nL49@P1: ● Do not reinforce harmful body ideals and behaviors through appearance ratings, body\nL50@P1: comparisons, or restrictive diet coaching.\nL51@P1: ● Incorporate safeguards that prevent recommendations for adults to initiate conversations\nL52@P1-2: with teens.● Respond to user queries in an age-appropriate manner that helps teens process issues\nL53@P2: without becoming a substitute for their therapist or best friend.\nL54@P2: In addition to developing U18 policies, AI companies should evaluate their implementation through\nL55@P2: testing prior to deployment and through monitoring and enforcement after deployment.\nL56@P2: 3. Defaulting to a safe U18 experience when there is doubt about a user’s age.\nL57@P2: We believe that AI companies should default to a safe U18 experience if there is doubt about a\nL58@P2: user’s age. Consistent with our mission to benefit all of humanity, OpenAI makes its products\nL59@P2: available for free. Some of our free users choose to use ChatGPT, for example, without logging in.\nL60@P2: In these instances, because it may not be possible to predict a user’s age, we will default to the\nL61@P2: U18 experience. We understand that this might hinder some adults’ experience using free products\nL62@P2: – but we are prioritizing kid and teen safety. After a user logs in – including to a free account – we\nL63@P2: will estimate the user’s age and provide them with an age-appropriate experience. We will also\nL64@P2: provide users with the ability to appeal our decision if they believe we made an error.\nL65@P2: 4. Empowering families with accessible parental controls.\nL66@P2: All AI systems should begin with robust default protections for teens. They should then provide\nL67@P2: parents and educators with additional layered protections that they can use to personalize and\nL68@P2: further support their teens’ experience. Parental controls like those now available in ChatGPT\nL69@P2: should be informed by research and provide parents with the tools and flexibility that they need to\nL70@P2: support their teen’s development in a way that works best for their family. We believe that all\nL71@P2: AI-related parental controls should allow parents to:\nL72@P2: ● Link their account with their teen’s account, if the teen is over 13, through a simple email\nL73@P2: invitation. ChatGPT is for individuals 13 years of age and older.\nL74@P2: ● Control how ChatGPT responds to their teen with age-appropriate model behavior rules,\nL75@P2: which are on by default.\nL76@P2: ● Manage privacy and data settings, including turning off memory and chat history to prevent\nL77@P2: the model from retaining details about past chats and enabling conversations to persist\nL78@P2: across multiple sessions.\nL79@P2: ● Receive alerts when their teen’s activity suggests an intent to harm themselves.\nL80@P2: ● Set blackout hours to ensure teens take breaks and spend time offline.\nL81@P2: ● Adjust feeds to manage their teen’s experience by opting into a non-personalized feed.\nL82@P2: 5. Designing for well-being by embedding features that are informed by research and\nL83@P2: that help people when they need it most.\nL84@P2: All AI companies should prioritize teen well-being by developing and deploying features that\nL85@P2-3: support teens’ mental health and wellness. These features should be informed by the latestresearch, guided by external experts, and updated as the industry learns more about best\nL86@P3: practices for facilitating safe experiences for teens using AI. Such safeguards should include:\nL87@P3: ● Notifying parents if their teen expresses suicidal intent. If the parents cannot be reached,\nL88@P3: notifying law enforcement and public safety officials if there is risk of imminent harm.\nL89@P3: ● Supporting users who express suicidal intent in seeking help and referring them to\nL90@P3: real-world resources such as 911 or 988 (the suicide and crisis hotline) in the US and\nL91@P3: findahelpline.com everywhere else.\nL92@P3: ● Notifying law enforcement if there is a credible threat of risk of harm to others.\nL93@P3: ● Collaborating with and supporting mental health and child safety organizations providing\nL94@P3: crisis response and interventions – especially organizations to which AI companies are\nL95@P3: directing their users.\nL96@P3: ● Surfacing reminders during long sessions to encourage breaks and healthy use.\nL97@P3: ● Supporting external research on mental health, emotional well-being, and child and teen\nL98@P3: development for AI users.\nL99@P3: ● Establishing advisory councils composed of external experts in mental health, well-being,\nL100@P3: and child development to advise on design and deployment choices.\nL101@P3: We remain committed to ensuring that our kids and teen safeguards will be open and guided by\nL102@P3: research. We will share what we learn, support independent research, and seek ongoing feedback\nL103@P3: from teens, parents, educators, and experts – including our Expert Council on Well-Being and AI –\nL104@P3: as we build and improve.\nL105@P3: We will also work with schools, teachers, and researchers to continually learn how we can enhance\nL106@P3: teens’ experience – allowing them to maximally benefit from using AI at home, at school and in\nL107@P3: their future careers – while being protected from potential harms.\nL108@P3: And, we remain committed to working with policymakers; child safety advocacy groups; parent,\nL109@P3: teacher, and community organizations; and other experts to craft public policies that promote the\nL110@P3: protections described above.\nL111@P3: About OpenAI\nL112@P3: Artificial intelligence is an innovation like electricity—it will change how we live, how we work, and how we\nL113@P3: engage with one another. OpenAI’s mission is to ensure that artificial general intelligence benefits all of\nL114@P3: humanity. We're building AI to help people solve hard problems because by helping with the hard problems,\nL115@P3: AI can benefit the most people possible—through more scientific discoveries, better healthcare and\nL116@P3: education, and improved productivity. We’re off to a strong start, creating freely available intelligence being\nL117@P3: used by more than 800 million people around the world, including 4 million developers. We believe AI will\nL118@P3: scale human ingenuity and drive unprecedented productivity, economic growth, and new freedoms that help\nL119@P3: people accomplish what we can't even imagine today.\n"